. Week dates are checked across years whose Jan 1 falls on different weekdays, and a few hundred dates are formatted and parsed back.\n- **Times and datetimes:** fractional hours, minutes and seconds, with both `,` and `.` as separators; long fractions are cut off, not rounded. Also all time-zone offset forms and `Z`, round trips, and error messages.\n- **Time zones:** parsing and formatting offsets in all three styles, `UTC`, `FixedOffset`, the local time zone (compared against Python's own `time` module), and the error for offsets of 100 hours or more.\n- **The `Duration` class:** adding and subtracting months and years from dates, including month-end clamping (Jan 31 + 1 month → Feb 29/28) and year rollover, plus fractional years/months being rejected. Also the leap-year helper (checked against `calendar` for 1890–2109), multiplication, negation, equality between `Duration` and `timedelta`, hashing, pickling, `str`/`repr`, and converting to a `timedelta` from a start or end date.\n- **Duration strings:** the standard `P…` form, weeks, signs, fractions, the alternative `PYYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss` form, wrong input types, and formatting back including negative durations.\n- **Formatting:** every format code, for dates, times and durations, plus all the predefined format constants.\n\n**Behaviours I found and didn't treat as bugs.** Six of my first expectations failed. Two were simply my own mistakes. The other four are lenient behaviours in the library, so I adjusted those tests to match rather than flag them. You may want to decide whether they are real bugs:\n- A month of `00` is accepted: `1985-00-10` silently becomes January 10.\n- `PT` and `P1YT` (nothing after the `T`) are accepted as durations instead of rejected.\n- Week-date formatting uses the calendar year rather than the ISO week year. For example, 2000-01-01 is written as `2000-W52-6`, which parses back to 2000-12-30. The round-trip test only checks dates where the two years agree.\n- A datetime using Python's built-in `timezone.utc` is written as `+00:00`, not `Z`; the library's own `UTC` gives `Z`.\n\n`mutmut` is installed and `pyproject.toml` is set up for it, but I didn't run it, so I haven't measured how many injected bugs the suite catches.","ttft_ms":2513,"type":"result","duration_ms":231305,"uuid":"2c1a893f-c370-447a-a1aa-75b4352c70ce","ttft_stream_ms":1335,"time_to_request_ms":439,"first_content_frame_ms":1335,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}

